X-ray data (Table S1) show that each $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster encompasses six crystallographically equivalent $\mu_{3}\mathrm{-O}^{2\ensuremath{-}}$ anions $(\mathrm{Zr-O}_{3}$ bond length, 2.133 Å), placed at the equatorial positions of the hexagon which are located $\mathbf{0.682}(6)$ Å above the plane containing the three $\mathrm{Zr}$ atoms. In addition, there are two crystallographically equivalent $\mu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}}^{-}$ anions $(\mathrm{{Zr}}\mathrm{{-}}\mathrm{{O}}_{2}}$ bond length, $2.187\mathrm{\AA})$ at the faces of the hexagon, placed $\mathbf{0.840(1)\AA}$ above the plane of the adjacent, three $Z\mathrm{r}$ atoms. Interestingly, the observed chemical formula, $\mathrm{[}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\mathrm{-O}})_{6}(\mu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}})_{2}\mathrm{]}^{10+}$ , representing the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ core, is different from the previously reported hexagonal $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters, formulated as $[Z\mathrm{r}_{6}(\tilde{\mu_{3}}{\mathrm{-OH}})_{8}]^{16\bar{+}}(\mathrm{P}\mathrm{\tilde{C}N}{\mathrm{-}}224)^{9\bar{+}}$ a, $[Z\mathbf{r}_{6}(\mu_{3}-$ $\mathrm{O)}_{4}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})_{4}]^{12+96}$ and $\mathrm{[Zr_{6}(\mu_{3}\mathrm{-O)_{8}(O H)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{10}]^{6+9C}}.}$ The $Z\mathrm{r}$ atoms at the faces of the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ hexagon complete their coordination sphere with acetate anions (Ac), $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ and $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ species. $\mathrm{^{1}H N M R}$ measurements in acetone exchanged samples, digested with ${\mathsf{N a O H}}$ , showed that the Ac:L ratio is $5$ (see Figure $\mathrm{S}_{12}$ ). Accordingly, the proposed charge balanced chemical formula for the pbz-MOF- $\mathbf{\cdot1}$ is $[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}\mathrm{-O})_{6}(\mu_{3}\mathrm{-OH})_{2}(\mathrm{Ac})_{5}(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]\mathrm{L}$ . The formula is also confirmed by thermogravimetric (TGA) and elemental analysis.
Further structural analysis of the pbz-MOF-1, revealed that the observed dihedral angle between the central phenyl ring of the organic linker and the hexagonal plane of the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster is $70.53^{\mathrm{{^{o}}}}$ (see Figure S9), as expected and imposed by this topology. In point of fact, given that there are no preassembled $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters as a starting entity in the initial reaction mixture, it is conceivable to regard the organic ligand as a directing-cluster agent and a template for the in situ formation of these 6-c clusters, prompted by the suitable geometrical features of the ligand favoring the assembly of the stable pbz-MOF, a highly symmetric cubic lattice.
The phase purity of pbz-MOF-1 was confirmed by comparison of the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns. The compound was found to be stable in common organic solvents including acetonitrile and acetone (Figure S7).
As shown in Figure 2c, the structure of pbz-MOF-1 contains tetrahedral cavities of approximately $\bar{13}\mathring\mathrm{A}$ in diameter, delimited by four organic linkers, occupying the faces of the tetrahedron, and twelve $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters. These cavities are interconnected through triangular-like windows and smaller cavities of approximately $6.5\overset{\mathrm{~\textstyle~-~}}{\mathrm{~A~}}$ and $9.5\mathring\mathrm{A}$ , respectively, running along the [110] crystallographic direction (Figure S10, S11). The corresponding solvent-accessible volume in the pbzMOF-1, calculated using the PLATON software17, is estimated to be $69\text{\textperthousand}$ . Looking along the [100] crystallographic direction, the distance between opposite $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters is approximately $3.8\mathring\mathrm{A}$ , measured from the methyl carbon atoms of the coordinate acetate anions and without taking into account the hydrogen atoms. Therefore, it is highly likely that the size of the hexagonal ligand in the pbz-MOF-1 represents a size cutoff for this type of linkers, below which the pbz net cannot be formed due to steric effects.
Argon adsorption isotherm recorded at $87\mathrm{K}$ confirmed the permanent porosity of pbz-MOF-1, showing a fully reversible type-I isotherm characteristic of a microporous material, see Figure 3a. The apparent BET (Langmuir) surface area, estimated using the pressure range 0.0065-0.0051 $\mathrm{P/P_{o}}$ (seven points), was estimated to be $2415\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}(2556\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ , in excellent agreement with the calculated BET surface area, $2432\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ , obtained by Poreblazer.18 The pore volume, calculated at ${\bf0.95\mathrm{P/P_{0}}}$ is 0.99 $\mathbf{cm}^{3}\mathbf{g}^{-1}.$ , is in excellent agreement with the crytallographically determined value of 1.0 $\mathbf{cm}^{3}\mathbf{g}^{\mathbf{-1}}$ . In addition to gas adsorption results, the structural integrity of the activated pbz-MOF-1 was supported by PXRD measurements (Figure S7). The pore size distribution was calculated from the Ar adsorption isotherm using NLDFT, revealing three distinct peaks centered at ${\bf1}_{3}\tilde{\bf A}_{:}$ , $9.5\mathring\mathrm{A}$ and $6.4\mathring\mathrm{A}$ (inset in Figure 3a), in full agreement with the crystallographic analysis.
In light of its interesting structural features containing a high density of phenyl rings per accessible volume (2.3 per $\scriptstyle\mathrm{nm}^{3},$ ), combined with a well-defined micropore system and high BET surface area $\left(2415\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)$ , pbz-MOF-1 may be considered as an excellent adsorbent candidate for gas storage applications and in particular for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . Accordingly, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption measurements at 112 K, revealed a reversible type-I isotherm (Figure $\mathrm{S}_{13}\mathrm{\dot{\Omega}}$ ), from which the
total pore volume at 0.99 $\mathrm{P/P_{o}}$ was calculated to be 0.95 $\mathbf{cm}^{3}\mathbf{g}^{\mathbf{-1}}$ , indicating that the full available pore space in the pbz-MOF-1 is readily accessible by $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecules. Highpressure $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption measurements were carried out at different temperatures up to 80 bar (Figure 3b). The observed gravimetric and volumetric uptake at 80 bar and 298 K is $\mathbf{o.23gg^{-1}}$ and 210. $_4\mathrm{cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , placing pbz-MOF-1 among the high performing MOFs for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage (Table $S_{2}$ and Figure $\mathrm{S14}$ ). The corresponding $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ capacities at $_{273}\mathrm{K}$ and $258\mathrm{K}$ are $\mathbf{0.279g^{-1}}/246\mathbf{cm}^{3}\mathbf{cm}^{-3}$ and $\mathbf{0.299gg^{-1}}/267\mathbf{cm}^{3}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , respectively. Further analysis of the adsorption uptakes at different pressures and room temperature showed that pbz-MOF-1 exhibits one of the largest increase in the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorbed phase density between 35 and $65$ bar $(38.3\%)$ , as compared to other well-performing MOFs for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage (Table $S_{3}$ and Figure S15). Reasonably, this enhanced $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ density in the pbz-MOF-1 could be associated with the favorable and very high efficiency in filling the available space in the pore network. Equivalent values for other existing
MOFs such as HKUST- $\mathbf{\cdot1}$ $\left(17.4\%\right)^{19}$ , Al-soc-MOF- $\mathbf{\cdot1}$ $\left(62.8\%\right)^{20}$ , $\mathsf{P C N-1}4$ $(16.6\%)^{21}$ , MOF- $520$ $(38.7\%)^{22}$ and MOF-905 (44.6 $\%)^{23}$ supports and affirms the high degree of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ packing in the pbz-MOF-1 between 35 and $65$ bar. The gravimetric and volumetric working capacity between $5^{-80}$ bar and $_{298\mathrm{K}}$ is estimated to be $22.7\mathrm{Wt\%}$ and $180\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , respectively. Interestingly, in contrast to other MOFs with intermediate porosity such as $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{PCN-1}}4^{21}$ , HKUST- $\cdot1^{19}$ , Ni-MOF- ${\cdot}74^{19}$ and UTSA- ${\cdot20}^{24}$ , the decrease of the storage temperature to $_{273}\mathrm{K}$ led to an increase in the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ working capacity. For instance, in the case HKUST- $\mathbf{\cdot1}$ and when going from 298 K to ${}_{273}\mathrm{K},$ , the volumetric working capacity between $5^{-65}$ bar decreased from 190 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ to $155\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ . Whereas for pbz-MOF-1, under the same conditions, the opposite occurred and an increase from $162\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{3}\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ to $185\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{3}\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ was observed. This manifest unique feature in the microporous pbz-MOF-1 could be attributed to the favorable synergistic effect between the pore system dimensions and the localized high density of aromatic rings per accessible unit volume.
In summary, we have demonstrated for the first time the successful use of reticular chemistry and the MMB approach for the construction of a highly connected $Z\mathbf{r}$ -based MOF with the intricate pbz topology. The synthesis of the pbzMOF-1, constructed from 6-connected hexagonal $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters and bridged by a novel nanosized hexatopic linker, represents the first example of a crystalline solid-state material displaying the 6-c pbz net. High pressure $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption measurements on the highly porous pbz-MOF-1 revealed a high storage capacity of $\mathbf{o.23gg^{-1}}$ and 210. $4\mathrm{cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP})\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at 80 bar. Moreover, pbz-MOF-1 exhibits one of the highest enhancement in the adsorbed phase density when going from 35 to $65$ bar. The successful synthesis of the first pbzMOF paves the way to expand this approach to other ligands and inorganic building blocks with the aim to tune the pore system and functionality.
